#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 6.6.17
# DESCRIPTION
# Find the derivative of a function using the chain rule

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of integration)
## DBsection(Work)
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## MLT(work_tank_picture)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('6.6')
## Problem1('17')
## KEYWORDS('work')
## RESOURCES('Anton_6_6_17.png')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");
Context()->variables->add(u => 'Real');

$a   = random(1, 2, 1);
$am1 = 3 - $a;
$h   = random(4, 20, 4);
$fh  = 4 * $h;

$Work = Compute("2*9810*$h/9*(27+2*$am1**3-9*$am1**2)");

BEGIN_PGML

The vat shown in the accompanying figure (click on graph to enlarge) has a triangular side of length 4 m and depth 3 m, and is [`\small{h=}`][$h] m long, contains water to a depth of [$a] m. Find the work required (in Joules) to pump all the water to the top of the vat. (Use 9810 [`\textrm{N/m}^3`] as the weight density of the water.)

>>[!A vat with a triangular cross section and vertex down.!]{'Anton_6_6_17.png'}{200}<<


    Work = [_]{$Work} Joules

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

 SOLUTION 

If we introduce a vertical [` \small{x}- `]axis, with the origin at the top of the vat, then the water lies between [`\small{ [$am1] \le x \le 3}`]. A strip of water at depth [`\small{x}`] and thickness [` \small{dx}`] has volume [`dV=(2r)([$h])(dx)`] where [`r`] is half of the strip's width. From similar triangles we see that [` \frac{2}{3}=\frac{r}{3-x}`], hence

[```
\begin{aligned}
dV & = \frac{[$fh]}{3}(3-x)dx \\
dF & = 9810 \frac{[$fh]}{3}(3-x)dx \qquad \text{(multiply by weight density to turn volume into weight or force)} \\
dW & = 9810\frac{[$fh]}{3}(3-x)x dx\qquad \text{(multiply force by distance to obtain work moving strip of water)}\\
\end{aligned}
```]

Therefore, to get the total work, we need to sum (integrate) over [`[$am1] \le x \le 3`].

[```\text{Work} =9810\frac{[$fh]}{3} \int_{[$am1]}^{3}(3-x)xdx = [$Work] \;\textrm{J}. ```]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
